    <section id="what">
      <h2>What Library Packs Are</h2>
      <p>A library pack is a reusable bundle of images with a title, description, and tags.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Use packs to group related assets like map sets, token kits, or encounter bundles.</li>
        <li>Packs do not duplicate your base image files; they organize references to images.</li>
        <li>Tags are pack-level metadata used for search and catalog readability.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="value">
      <h2>Why Library Packs Matter</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Share installable resources:</strong> curate once, publish once, then let other users and Wyrlds install immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Faster prep for everyone:</strong> avoid rebuilding the same encounter assets repeatedly.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistency across games:</strong> keep map/token sets aligned across tables and campaigns.</li>
        <li><strong>Owner convenience too:</strong> creators can still use their own packs directly in table sidebars.</li>
        <li><strong>Cleaner libraries:</strong> separate ad-hoc uploads from durable, reusable content bundles.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="scope">
      <h2>Library Types</h2>
      <p>Every library screen is in exactly one library type: User library or Wyrld library.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>User library:</strong> personal account context.</li>
        <li><strong>Wyrld library:</strong> shared manager/editor context for that Wyrld.</li>
        <li><strong>Owned packs</strong> are library-specific: User library owns user packs; Wyrld library owns that Wyrld's packs.</li>
        <li>You can still install packs across libraries, including your own packs from another library.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="discover">
      <h2>What Discover Means</h2>
      <p>Discover is the community resource-sharing catalog.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Discover shows published packs that are available to your current library.</li>
        <li>Use preview before install to verify pack quality and contents.</li>
        <li>Install adds that pack to the current library's installed list so it is usable in that library.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="owned-installed">
      <h2>Owned vs Installed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Owned:</strong> packs created by this exact library.</li>
        <li><strong>Installed:</strong> packs attached to this library for use, regardless of who published them.</li>
        <li>Uninstall removes the pack from the current library install list.</li>
        <li>Delete Pack removes the pack itself (and installs that depend on it).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="publish">
      <h2>Publishing and Visibility</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Private / Wyrld visibility</strong> keeps packs in non-public access paths.</li>
        <li><strong>Public Pro + Published</strong> makes a pack discoverable for community use under pro gating.</li>
        <li>Tags improve discoverability because pack search matches title, description, and tags.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="workflow">
      <h2>Recommended Workflow</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Create a pack in your current library.</li>
        <li>Add images and useful tags.</li>
        <li>Optionally publish when ready for community sharing.</li>
        <li>Use Discover to find and preview other creators' packs.</li>
        <li>Install packs into the library where you want to use them.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="permissions">
      <h2>Permissions and Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Pack tooling is tied to pro capabilities in the active library context.</li>
        <li>Sandbox mode disables discovery and install/uninstall management for packs.</li>
        <li>Wyrld library actions follow Wyrld editor/manager permissions.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
